    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 21st with a limited but high-upside slate, anchored by **Kazuma Okamoto** and **Dillon Dingler**. **Kazuma Okamoto** earns the day's highest mark at 65.31; facing Rod\u00f3n, **Kazuma Okamoto** benefits significantly from a platoon advantage and a favorable park environment that elevates his base projection. Similarly, **Dillon Dingler** is a strong play at 60.31, capitalizing on a dynamic platoon edge against Cantillo. The move to the #3 spot in the order provides the necessary multiplier to push **Dillon Dingler** into our recommended start tier, ensuring we maximize output from the catcher position. **Carson Benge** sits just on the fringe of the elite threshold at 57.23; while his lead-off role against Cavalli is promising, he remains a marginal play that highlights our current reliance on the top of the order to drive run production in the absence of our core sluggers.\n\nOur bench situation is dictated largely by team off-days for heavy hitters like **Yordan Alvarez**, **Junior Caminero**, **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, and **Trea Turner**, all of whom are unavailable today. This attrition forces a heavy rotation reliance on players like **Kyle Schwarber**, **Christian Yelich**, **Caleb Durbin**, and **Hyeseong Kim**. Looking ahead, the scarcity of stolen bases remains the primary weight in our long-term strategy, meaning that once **Pete Crow-Armstrong** or **Caleb Durbin** return to active status, we will aggressively pivot away from lower-efficiency bats like **Colt Keith**, **Zac Veen**, or **Lars Nootbaar**. We continue to monitor the utility bench\u2014specifically **Chase Meidroth**, **Gary Sanchez**, **Luke Raley**, **Josh Smith**, **Casey Schmitt**, **Pedro Ramirez**, and **Luis Pena**\u2014for any late-breaking lineup changes that might create a platoon opportunity, though for today, the current starters represent the highest ceiling for our daily efficiency targets.",
